# Iluminate Designer Upgrade Plan

**Reading policy:** supporting regression and decision history. Do not read this
entire document for routine Designer work. Current behavior is summarized in
`.agent/DESIGNER_HANDOFF.md`, and current interaction rules live in
`.agent/DESIGNER_UX_CONTRACT.md`.

**Status:** canonical working plan; phases 0–10 implemented, operator-software round-trip pending field acceptance  
**Created:** 2026-10-05  
**Scope:** geometry authoring, Face Graphic, manufacturing preparation and vector export  
**Rule:** keep this document aligned whenever the upgrade scope, sequencing or architecture changes.

## Implementation checkpoint — 2026-10-05

Phases 0–2 are implemented as a deliberately UI-neutral architectural upgrade:

- schema `designerSchemaVersion: 2` and canonical `designer.geometries` records;
- stable `geometryId` references from build areas, zones and channels;
- backward-compatible normalization of legacy inline geometry;
- canonical geometry wins when a saved v2 document loads, while all current
  editing mutations commit through `canonicalizeDesignerGeometry`;
- pure common geometry command boundary;
- centralized layer/tool policy for every existing geometric drawing tool;
- explicit geometry and electrical engine APIs over the shared Paper canvas;
- regression coverage for migration, authority, commands and tool dispatch.

During phase 2, inline bounds/points remain in semantic objects as a compatibility
view so the existing renderer and interaction code can remain visually stable.
They are synchronized at the mutation boundary and rebuilt from canonical
geometry on load; they are not a second editorial authority. Removing that
view is a later internal cleanup, after dependent modules consume geometry IDs
directly.

Phase 3 adds the first user-visible result of that foundation:

- a native `Face Graphic` canvas category and persisted layer settings;
- rectangle, ellipse, polygon and Bezier tools routed through the common
  geometry policy;
- native Face Graphic objects with canonical `geometryId` references;
- `opaque`, `clear` and `translucent` pass modes;
- translucent filter color, defaulting to `#FFFFFF`;
- selection, ordering, visibility, layer lock, object lock, copy/paste, delete
  and node editing;
- explicit exclusion from the electrical compile signature and pixelMap.

The Design canvas uses a magenta fabrication preview for these objects. The
actual front-light buffer filter was added in phase 8; phase 3 did not
introduce a naive overlay into Animate.

Phase 4 adds associative `Project Geometry` between geometric layers:

- the projection stores a stable reference to canonical source geometry rather
  than duplicating it;
- source edits are reflected immediately in the target layer;
- projected geometry is cyan, dashed and read-only on the canvas;
- `Break Link` materializes an independent object in Reference, Diffusors or
  Face Graphic;
- deleting a source preserves a selectable broken reference that can be
  relinked;
- direct and chained dependency cycles are rejected;
- projections remain outside the electrical compile signature and pixelMap.

The command is intentionally contextual: select a geometric object, choose the
target layer, then use `Project Geometry`. Selecting a projection exposes its
source, status and `Break Link`. This keeps projection as an object operation,
not as a second drawing tool.

Phase 5 adds compound paths and destructive boolean commands to the shared
geometry engine:

- canonical paths may persist multiple closed contours with `evenodd` fill;
- holes affect rendering, canvas hit testing and zone pixel membership;
- Shift/Ctrl/Command-click builds a same-layer geometric selection;
- `Union`, `Subtract`, `Intersect` and `Exclude` resolve through Paper.js and
  create one native result in the selected layer;
- native input objects are consumed atomically while projected operands remain
  linked construction references;
- closed-profile, self-intersection and duplicate-contour validation blocks an
  invalid boolean result;
- zone topology participates in the Compile signature and invalid profiles are
  reported as compile errors.

`Subtract` is order-sensitive: the first selected profile is the base and later
profiles are removed from it. Compound contour nodes are intentionally not
edited individually in this phase; the complete result can be moved or resized.

Phase 6 adds associative fabrication transforms over canonical or projected
geometry:

- `Offset Path` creates a persisted derived profile with a signed distance in
  millimeters and `round`, `miter` or `bevel` joins;
- miter offsets expose their limit and fall back deterministically when that
  limit is exceeded;
- `Fillet` creates a persisted derived profile with a radius in millimeters and
  may affect all corners or an explicit one-based corner list;
- excessive radii clamp to the available neighboring legs and surface a
  warning instead of silently producing invalid geometry;
- derived profiles remain live through shallow acyclic chains such as
  source → projection → offset → fillet;
- holes and compound contours are preserved, while collapse, invalid topology,
  broken sources and cycles remain visible recoverable states;
- `Break Link` materializes the current result as independent native geometry.

Derived profiles render as amber construction geometry and remain electrically
neutral until materialized into a native semantic object. Their implementation
is shared by every geometric layer; layer components must not reproduce offset
or fillet calculations.

Phase 7 adds controlled editable text and deterministic outlines:

- Reference, Diffusors and Face Graphic reuse one contextual Text tool;
- `designer.texts` persists content, target layer, controlled font ID and hash,
  size in millimeters, tracking, line height, alignment and position;
- the initial controlled catalog contains versioned Iluminate Sans regular and
  bold resources and never substitutes a browser/OS font;
- single-line and basic multiline text render on canvas, remain selectable,
  movable, lockable, visible and editable after save/reload;
- `Convert to paths` verifies the font SHA-256 before generating normal compound
  Bezier geometry with even-odd glyph holes;
- conversion atomically replaces editable text with a native Reference, Zone or
  Face Graphic profile, so immediate Undo restores the text;
- the outline generator applies the font's glyph advances and kerning plus the
  persisted tracking/alignment values and is covered by deterministic tests.

Text itself remains electrically neutral. Converting text in Diffusors creates
a normal Zone whose resulting geometry participates in Compile and pixel
membership exactly like any other native zone.

## 1. Objective

Evolve the current Designer into the single source of truth for the illuminated
sign and its fabrication geometry, without turning Iluminate into a general
purpose vector illustration application.

The intended workflow is:

```text
Iluminate Designer
-> visual and fabrication geometry
-> zones and lighting construction
-> electrical strings and controller topology
-> optical simulation with Face Graphic
-> validated vector export
-> cutter/router operator software
```

This should replace avoidable round trips such as:

```text
Fusion 360 -> Inkscape -> cutter software -> manual corrections
```

Iluminate will export open vector interchange files. It will not generate STL,
machine-specific binary cutting files, toolpaths or CNC programs.

## 2. Non-Negotiable Decisions

- `iluminate.partituras.document_json` remains the only editable source of truth.
- Paper.js remains the interactive canvas/view adapter; Paper.js project JSON is
  never persisted as the domain model.
- The canvas, coordinate system, viewport, zoom, pan, snapping, selection and
  Undo/Redo infrastructure are shared.
- Geometry authoring and electrical routing are separate domain engines over
  that shared canvas.
- Improvements to Pen/Bezier/node editing are implemented once in the geometry
  engine and become available to every geometric layer whose policy permits them.
- Electrical tools remain exclusive to Strings/Hardware where appropriate.
- `Face Graphic` is a real canvas layer with native vector geometry. It is not
  an imported image and is not an effect target.
- A Face Graphic region controls only final frontal light passage:
  `opaque`, `clear` or `translucent`.
- Face Graphic does not own diffusion, LED gap, intensity, softness, throw,
  beam angle, strings or effects. Those remain responsibilities of zones and
  their Lighting Setups.
- Face Graphic never changes electrical topology, `pixelMap`, zone membership,
  clip targeting or the firmware artifact.
- Manufacturing export contains geometry, physical scale, layers and material
  separation. Cutter/router configuration and tool compensation remain the
  operator's responsibility.

## 3. Target Architecture

```text
Shared Designer Canvas
├── viewport / coordinates / rulers
├── zoom / pan / snapping
├── selection / hit dispatch
├── tool registry
├── Undo / Redo command boundary
└── Paper.js adapter

Domain engines
├── Geometry engine
│   ├── paths and compound paths
│   ├── rectangles / ellipses / polygons
│   ├── Bezier nodes and handles
│   ├── text and text-to-path
│   ├── associative projections
│   ├── offsets
│   ├── fillets
│   ├── boolean operations
│   └── fabrication validation / vector export
│
└── Electrical engine
    ├── controller ports
    ├── data cables
    ├── LED strings
    ├── DIN / DOUT terminals
    ├── solder joints
    ├── route cutting
    ├── joint detachment
    ├── electrical topology
    └── serial traversal / pixelMap input
```

The engines share coordinates and canvas services, but neither engine may
interpret or mutate the other engine's domain objects.

### 3.1 Tool dispatch

```text
Artwork      -> geometric/reference tools
Diffusors    -> geometric tools and Channel
Face Graphic -> geometric and fabrication tools
Strings      -> electrical route tools
Hardware     -> controller selection/configuration
```

Clipboard commands may be common at the UI level, but duplication semantics
are owned by each engine. In particular, duplicating an electrical route must
not copy solder joints or controller/output connectivity accidentally.

### 3.2 Cut operations are not interchangeable

```text
Split geometric path
-> divides vector geometry

Cut electrical route
-> creates or changes terminals
-> breaks electrical continuity
-> reconciles joints
-> may change serial topology
```

The electrical scissors workflow remains in the electrical engine.

## 4. Canonical Geometry Model

Introduce a shared persisted geometry representation. Semantic objects should
reference geometry rather than each keeping incompatible path implementations.

Conceptual shape:

```ts
type DesignerGeometry = {
  id: string;
  kind: "rect" | "ellipse" | "path";
  points: DesignerPoint[];
  closed: boolean;
  fillRule: "nonzero" | "evenodd";
  transform: DesignerTransform;
  derivedOperation?: DesignerDerivedOperation;
};
```

Consumers:

```text
Zone                -> geometryId
Build Area          -> geometryId
Channel             -> geometryId + width/topology
Face Graphic region -> geometryId + passMode/filterColor
```

Electrical routes remain separate because their nodes, terminals, direction
and connectivity have different semantics.

The persisted geometry in `document_json` is the editorial truth. The geometry
engine is the sole deterministic interpreter and transformer of that data.
Paper.js renders it and translates pointer events into commands.

## 5. Face Graphic Contract

Face Graphic is the top physical definition layer placed over the front face
of a light box, light chamber or dimensional letter.

Conceptual object:

```ts
type DesignerFaceGraphicElement = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  geometryId: string;
  passMode: "opaque" | "clear" | "translucent";
  filterColor: string;
  visible: boolean;
  locked: boolean;
};
```

Defaults:

```text
passMode: translucent
filterColor: #FFFFFF
```

Optical behavior:

```text
opaque      -> output = 0
clear       -> output = computed frontal light
translucent -> output = computed frontal light × filterColor
```

The operation is a color transmission/filter operation, not a repaint. White
light through red translucent vinyl becomes red. Blue light through an ideal
red filter becomes nearly black.

Face Graphic must not expose:

- LED or pixel density;
- gap or source distance;
- intensity;
- softness or diffusion;
- beam direction or beam angle;
- falloff or throw;
- strings;
- effects or clips.

Editor visibility/opacity controls are presentation aids and must not become
physical transmission controls.

Face Graphic changes:

- mark `document_json` dirty;
- invalidate the optical preview;
- invalidate fabrication export;
- do not invalidate electrical Compile;
- do not modify `generated_json` for firmware.

## 6. Associative Project Geometry

Support a Fusion-like `Project Geometry` workflow between geometric layers.

```text
Select source geometry on another layer
-> Project Geometry into active layer
-> linked, read-only projected reference
```

Conceptual record:

```ts
type DesignerProjection = {
  id: string;
  sourceGeometryId: string;
  targetLayer: DesignerLayerId;
  linked: boolean;
};
```

Rules:

- Linked projection resolves from its source; it is not a second authoritative
  copy of the path.
- Changes to the source update the projection.
- Projected geometry has a distinct canvas presentation.
- `Break Link` materializes an independent editable geometry object.
- Source deletion produces a recoverable broken-reference issue.
- Relinking must be possible.
- Dependency cycles are invalid.
- Undo/Redo treats project, relink and break-link as complete commands.
- A projected reference is excluded from fabrication output unless it is used
  by a fabricable derived profile or is explicitly materialized.

Derived operation example:

```text
Zone geometry
-> linked projection into Face Graphic
-> outward offset
-> corner fillets
-> fabricable Face Graphic profile
```

## 7. Geometry Operations

### 7.1 Compound paths and boolean operations

Required before reliable mask/cut export:

- compound paths;
- holes;
- even-odd fill behavior;
- union;
- subtract;
- intersect;
- exclude;
- closed-profile validation;
- self-intersection detection;
- duplicate contour detection.

These operations allow letters with counters (`A`, `O`, `P`, `R`), opaque
backgrounds with illuminated windows and composed logos.

### 7.2 Offset Path

Required controls:

- inward/outward distance in millimeters;
- `round`, `miter` and `bevel` joins;
- miter limit;
- live preview;
- support for open and closed paths;
- preservation of holes and compound paths;
- deterministic handling of self-intersections;
- clear failure/warning behavior when an inward offset collapses a profile.

Offset is a design/fabrication operation. Machine tool compensation remains in
the cutter/router CAM workflow.

### 7.3 General fillets

Generalize the existing Channel-node `radiusMm` concept:

- apply to one or several eligible corners;
- radius in millimeters;
- live preview;
- clamp when the requested radius does not fit;
- warn when a fillet consumes a short neighboring leg;
- persist parametrically;
- recalculate when upstream projected geometry changes.

Dogbone and T-bone relief may be added later for CNC-fit workflows. They are
not required for the first fillet release.

### 7.4 Derived-operation graph

Operations should remain editable rather than immediately destroying their
inputs:

```text
source -> projection -> offset -> fillet -> resolved profile
```

The first version needs only a shallow acyclic dependency graph, not a complete
Fusion-style feature timeline.

## 8. Text And Text-To-Path

Editable text concept:

```ts
type DesignerText = {
  id: string;
  text: string;
  fontId: string;
  fontSizeMm: number;
  tracking: number;
  lineHeight: number;
  alignment: "left" | "center" | "right";
  transform: DesignerTransform;
};
```

Requirements:

- Do not depend on fonts installed in the operator's browser/OS.
- Use a controlled font resource with a stable identifier and content hash.
- Preserve deterministic glyph shaping and kerning.
- `Convert to paths` produces normal compound Bezier geometry.
- Conversion must preserve glyph holes.
- Conversion is reversible through immediate Undo.
- Manufacturing export converts all remaining text to outlines.
- Saved/reloaded text must produce identical outlines.

Initial scope:

- controlled Iluminate font catalog;
- single-line and basic multiline text;
- size, tracking, line height and alignment;
- conversion to paths.

Deferred:

- client font upload and licensing workflow;
- text on path;
- advanced typographic layout;
- automatic script-font welding.

## 9. Rendering Pipeline

Refactor Animate optical rendering into explicit buffers:

```text
Rear/external buffer
├── Halo
└── Wall Wash

Front-light buffer
├── front material/acrylic presentation
└── all Front light sources

Face Graphic pass
└── applies opaque/clear/translucent only to front-light buffer

Final composition
├── workspace/background
├── rear/external buffer
├── filtered front-light buffer
└── editor selection/outlines
```

A naive top-level multiply sprite is invalid because it would also filter the
background, Halo and Wall Wash.

Required deterministic visual cases:

- white light under red translucent graphic -> red;
- red light under red translucent graphic -> red;
- blue light under red translucent graphic -> nearly off;
- clear region -> unchanged light;
- opaque region -> fully blocked light;
- Halo and Wall Wash -> unaffected by Face Graphic;
- multiple zones -> continuous spatial mask alignment;
- zoom/pan -> no mask drift.

`As built` includes Face Graphic. `LED map` continues to expose the raw mapped
pixel output for diagnostics.

## 10. Manufacturing Export

### 10.1 SVG first

SVG is the first canonical interchange export because it matches the web/vector
model and is accepted by modern sign/cutting workflows.

Requirements:

- physical scale 1:1;
- explicit millimeter dimensions;
- stable `viewBox`;
- paths/compound paths only for manufacturing geometry;
- text converted to paths;
- grouped output by Designer layer and Face Graphic pass/color;
- exclude guides, projected construction references, selection visuals,
  electrical routes and controller graphics unless explicitly requested in a
  separate fabrication drawing;
- project/partitura identity, generation time and source checksum metadata.

### 10.2 DXF second

DXF supports path-oriented router/CAM interchange.

Requirements:

- explicit units;
- named layers by operation/material;
- deterministic Bezier/spline flattening tolerance when the destination
  workflow requires polylines;
- no raster content;
- no machine toolpaths.

### 10.3 Later compatibility

- vector PDF;
- EPS for older sign/cutter software;
- no proprietary AI/CDR generation;
- no STL;
- no machine-specific binary cutting format.

### 10.4 Export validation

Block or warn on:

- open contours where a closed profile is required;
- self-intersections;
- duplicate contours;
- invalid holes;
- geometry outside the fabrication area;
- features smaller than configured fabrication guidance;
- broken projection/derived-operation references;
- unresolved text/font resources;
- invalid or missing units;
- empty output.

## 11. Work Phases

### Phase 0 — Contract and regression baseline — COMPLETE

- Finalize architecture and schema decisions.
- Add `designerSchemaVersion` or equivalent migration discriminator.
- Capture legacy/current document fixtures.
- Add regression coverage for current geometric and electrical workflows.
- Record current Designer smoke scenarios.

Exit criteria:

- Existing Designer behavior is reproducibly covered before refactoring.

### Phase 1 — Internal separation with no UX change — COMPLETE

- Extract shared canvas services.
- Extract geometry-domain operations.
- Extract electrical-domain operations.
- Introduce layer-aware tool dispatch.
- Preserve current Paper.js presentation and behavior.

Exit criteria:

- Current tests/build/smoke pass with no intended UI behavior change.

### Phase 2 — Canonical geometry and commands — COMPLETE

- Introduce canonical geometry records and geometry identifiers.
- Make zones/build areas/channels consume the shared model.
- Add common geometry commands.
- Add layer policies/factories.
- Migrate legacy inline geometry on normalization/save.

Exit criteria:

- One Pen/Bezier implementation edits every geometric layer.
- Existing documents reopen without loss.

### Phase 3 — Face Graphic authoring layer — COMPLETE

- Add the Face Graphic category and layer settings.
- Add native geometric Face Graphic objects.
- Reuse shared geometric tools.
- Add pass mode and filter color.
- Add layer/object selection, ordering, lock and visibility.

Exit criteria:

- Face Graphic persists and edits without affecting Compile or wiring.

### Phase 4 — Associative Project Geometry — COMPLETE

- Add linked projections between geometric layers.
- Add visual distinction and read-only behavior.
- Add Break Link, relink and broken-reference handling.
- Add cycle detection.

Exit criteria:

- Editing source geometry updates the target-layer projection.

### Phase 5 — Compound paths and booleans — COMPLETE

- Add holes and fill rules.
- Add union/subtract/intersect/exclude.
- Add topology validation.

Exit criteria:

- Letter counters and opaque masks with transparent windows resolve correctly.

### Phase 6 — Offset and fillets — COMPLETE

- Add robust offset operation and join styles.
- Generalize parametric fillets.
- Add derived-operation recalculation and failure reporting.

Exit criteria:

- A projected zone can become an offset, filleted, fabricable profile.

### Phase 7 — Text and outlines — COMPLETE

- Add controlled font resources.
- Add editable text tool/object.
- Add deterministic text-to-path conversion.
- Add text/path persistence and Undo/Redo coverage.

Exit criteria:

- Text produces identical manufacturing geometry across save/reload/export.

### Phase 8 — Face Graphic optical renderer — COMPLETE

- Split rear/external and front-light buffers.
- Render Face Graphic vector masks.
- Apply pass/filter semantics only to frontal illumination.
- Preserve raw LED-map diagnostics.

Exit criteria:

- All canonical optical cases pass without changing Halo/Wall Wash behavior.

Implementation checkpoint (2026-10-06): Animate now builds explicit
rear/external and front-light Pixi containers. A full-canvas vector
transmission texture applies `opaque`, `clear` and RGB `translucent` semantics
only to the front container; compound paths retain their holes, overlaps obey
the persisted front-to-back order, and the uncovered face is opaque whenever
a Face Graphic exists. Layer/object visibility and opacity remain editor aids
and do not alter physical transmission. Direct-mounted Front LEDs are included
in the filtered buffer. LED Map bypasses the optical pass and remains raw.

### Phase 9 — Fabrication export — COMPLETE

- Implement SVG generation and validation.
- Add downloadable export UX.
- Implement DXF after SVG is stable.
- Add PDF/EPS only from validated real operator requirements.

Exit criteria:

- Export opens at 1:1 scale in selected cutter/router software and contains
  only intended manufacturing geometry.

Implementation checkpoint (2026-10-06): the Designer exports selected
Reference/substrate, Diffusors and Face Graphic fabrication categories through
one validated canonical pipeline. SVG uses explicit millimeter dimensions,
stable `viewBox`, path-only geometry, compound fill rules, native Beziers,
material/pass groups and identity/time/source-checksum metadata. Editable text
is deterministically outlined during export without mutating the document.
DXF declares millimeter units and named operation/material layers, and flattens
ellipses/Beziers to closed `LWPOLYLINE` entities with an operator-controlled,
deterministic tolerance. Artwork, linked construction projections, controller,
routes and pixels are excluded. Blocking diagnostics cover invalid/open/self-
intersecting/duplicate geometry, invalid compound contours, unresolved text,
broken derived operations, invalid units/tolerance and empty output; out-of-
bounds and small-feature guidance remain warnings. PDF/EPS remain deferred.

### Phase 10 — Integration and documentation — COMPLETE

- Complete old/new document migration coverage.
- Complete end-to-end electrical and geometry regression.
- Update all affected `.agent` and `docs` contracts.
- Regenerate generated context after the structural change is complete.

Exit criteria:

- Production build and full Designer smoke pass.
- Documentation and executable behavior agree.

Implementation checkpoint (2026-10-06): legacy and current Designer documents
now normalize without restoring deleted sample zones or routes; explicitly
empty authored collections remain empty, while fields absent from genuinely
older documents still receive compatible defaults. Current schema documents
are normalization-idempotent across save/reload, including canonical compound
geometry, projections, derived operations, Face Graphic, text and electrical
routes. Live offset/fillet operations targeting Face Graphic now persist their
own mask pass/color, participate in the As built frontal-light filter, retain
those semantics when materialized and export into the matching SVG/DXF material
group. They remain electrically neutral and never filter Halo or Wall Wash.

The deterministic Designer regression now contains 68 tests across 19 suites,
including an integrated geometry -> linked projection -> offset -> fillet ->
red translucent Face Graphic -> electrical compile -> save/reload -> SVG/DXF
scenario, plus controller/cable movement and detach behavior. The production
build, TypeScript validation, container deployment and HTTP/API smoke passed on
2026-10-06. Opening the interchange in each operator's chosen proprietary
cutter or router software remains an operational acceptance check rather than
an automated repository test.

## 12. Required Regression Coverage

### Geometry

- Create/edit rectangle, ellipse, polygon and Bezier.
- Insert/delete nodes.
- Edit handles and node types.
- Move/resize/duplicate/delete.
- Compound paths and holes.
- Boolean operations.
- Offset corner cases.
- Fillet limits.
- Text conversion.
- Projection update/break/relink/cycle rejection.
- Save/reload and Undo/Redo.

### Electrical

- Controller-port to data-cable solder.
- Cable/string and same-kind joints.
- Controller dragging with connected cables.
- Route/joint dragging.
- Cut route.
- Detach joint.
- Delete-route cleanup.
- Electrical traversal and serial order.
- Compile errors/warnings and generated pixelMap.

### Renderer

- Front/halo/wall-wash baseline behavior.
- Face Graphic opaque/clear/translucent behavior.
- Multiple zones and overlapping front sources.
- Pan/zoom/resize alignment.
- Preview regeneration while timeline playback is active.

### Export

- Unit and scale checks.
- Path/compound-path fidelity.
- Text outlines.
- Layer/material grouping.
- Open/self-intersecting/duplicate path diagnostics.
- SVG round-trip through representative cutter software.
- DXF round-trip through representative router/CAM software.

## 13. End-To-End Acceptance Scenario

1. Create editable text.
2. Convert the text to compound paths.
3. Use the geometry as a visual zone.
4. Project it associatively into Face Graphic.
5. Apply an outward offset.
6. Apply fabrication fillets.
7. Mark the resulting Face Graphic region as red translucent vinyl.
8. Draw controller, data cable and LED string topology.
9. Compile the physical layout.
10. Apply a white effect to the corresponding lighting target.
11. Confirm that only the Face Graphic opening is illuminated and appears red.
12. Confirm that Halo/Wall Wash, if present, are not filtered.
13. Edit the source geometry and confirm that the linked projection and derived
    operations update.
14. Break the projection link and edit the resulting independent geometry.
15. Export SVG and DXF at physical 1:1 scale.
16. Open the export in representative cutter/router operator software.
17. Reopen a pre-upgrade partitura and confirm no geometry or wiring loss.
18. Cut/detach an electrical route and confirm that vector-engine changes did
    not alter electrical semantics.

## 14. Validation Commands

At minimum for each implementation slice:

```bash
git diff --check
docker compose up -d --build iluminate-web
```

Run the web Designer test suite and add focused deterministic tests for every
new geometry operation. Continue using the Designer API/manual smoke described
in `.agent/EXECUTION_MAP.md`.

If the lighting-core package receives shared fabrication/geometry contracts,
also run:

```bash
docker run --rm -v "$PWD/services/lighting-core:/work" -w /work node:22-alpine sh -c "npm ci && npm test"
```

## 15. Documentation Updates Required During Implementation

Keep these files aligned as phases land:

- `.agent/RULES.md` when precedence or mandatory context changes;
- `.agent/DESIGNER_UX_CONTRACT.md` for the new Face Graphic layer/tools;
- `.agent/DESIGNER_HANDOFF.md` for actual Designer behavior and modules;
- `.agent/ILUMINATE_UI_STANDARDS.md` for fabrication UI conventions;
- `.agent/PIXELMAP_COMPOSER_DIRECTION.md` for geometry/Face Graphic boundaries;
- `.agent/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md` for completed phases;
- `.agent/AI_CONTEXT_LED_ORCHESTRATION_PLATFORM.md` for product architecture;
- `docs/partitura-lifecycle.md` for authoring, preview and invalidation rules.

After a major structural or context-inventory change, regenerate the compact
manifests (they are not routine startup context):

- `.agent/BRAIN_MAP.md`;
- `.agent/AI_CONTEXT_PACK.md`.

## 16. Scope Rejection Checklist

Reject or explicitly defer work that would turn this upgrade into:

- a general illustration/painting application;
- a desktop publishing system;
- a photomanipulation suite;
- a complete CAD constraint solver;
- a CAM/toolpath generator;
- a nesting/production-optimization suite in the first release;
- an STL/3D manufacturing pipeline;
- a machine-driver or proprietary cutter-file generator.

The accepted scope is precise 2D sign-fabrication geometry integrated with
Iluminate lighting design, electrical topology and optical simulation.
